import{_ as n,P as i,d as o,e as a,y as r,q as s,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.3 HV03 Orientation Anchor (Full Interface Card)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.3 HV03 Orientation Anchor (Full Interface Card)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1133,"xkLessons":{"A.3":{"title":"A.3 HV03 Orientation anchor (complete interface card)","lead":"HV03 retains sourced expressions of meaning and heterogeneous goals; only support for a preselected H1 outcome permits registration of a bounded effective anchor. Effects prove neither shared inner states nor goal justification.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3786–3851; original site location /theory/appendix-a/a-03#section-a-3. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research drafts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","Short quotations translated from Chinese: “candidate meaning materials” precede “conditionally effective orientation anchors.” Goals, identities, memories, commitments, fears, and common problems may all be sourced meaning materials, but how they alter resources, actions, or coordination requires separate evidence. A widespread slogan is not necessarily effective, still less proof of identical inner states or a justified goal. [T-main]","HV03-R0-candidate-anchor requires traceable expressions and forms of carriage without an eligible H1-instance yet, permitting registration of heterogeneous expressions, representational disputes, and H1 supplementary-evidence needs. Pause conditions prohibit R1 registration when there are only slogans, interpreter projection, or coerced agreement, while explicitly retaining R0 registration of sourced expressions, heterogeneity, and coercion risks. Insufficient strong support cannot be rewritten as no materials to analyze. [T-main]","HV03-R1-effective-anchor requires a preregistered H1-instance to obtain supported on the uniquely preselected criterion among resource allocation, action choice, or coordination outcomes, adding CAUSAL and E4. The outcome family among these three must be selected beforehand: if resources do not change, one cannot switch to actions, then select a favorable coordination measure when actions do not change. If multiple goals, models, or comparison rules need combining, freeze them beforehand too, rather than fishing for significance after results. [T-main；T-H1-contract]","R1 supports a conditional anchor only for that instance, selected outcome family, scale, and window. It does not extrapolate to unselected outcomes, imply genuine consent or unified inner states, or justify coercive meaning. An effective direction of coordination may arise from fear or pressure, making the distinction between established effects and normative justification especially important. Unresolved/unsupported results that fail the positive check also cannot declare meaning incapable of affecting any outcome without passing the null-conclusion check. [T-main；T-H1-contract]","Across nine axes, preserve differences between original expressions, action sequences, distributions of positions, and summaries. Aggregation requires added representation rules, heterogeneity, cost/benefit distributions, and J. Different goals at low-safety positions cannot be erased by majority summaries; silence, coerced agreement, and internal competition enter uncertainty and local exclusion zones. Different people may use the same words while referring to different costs and actions. [T-main]","Time windows distinguish short-term slogans, long-term commitments, and intergenerational memories. Outputs may enter events, mobilization for uptake, and normative agendas, but those downstream uses cannot inherit an effect not yet proved. Those claiming representativeness and those allocating resources on that basis have separate recording responsibilities; the interface authorizes neither unified meaning, representation of wishes, nor coordinated action. [T-main]","Appeals permit denial of representativeness, presentation of heterogeneous goals, refusal to be anchored, and review outside the original judgment chain. Authorized rollback must actually revoke naming and its representative/downstream coordination effects, restore heterogeneous-expression status, and retain versions and verification; it cannot compress public opinions into another new slogan. [T-main]","The verifiable original 1990 ADA signing statement publicly invokes liberty, equality, and commitments to administrative implementation. This is sourced expressive material, but it does not itself provide a preselected-outcome effect relative to comparison conditions. The statement alone cannot establish H1 supported or universal public consent. Real historical materials need not be denied significance, but inference ceilings must remain. [P-ADA；T-main]","Field-by-field study of all 39 fields (“Original card” below retains the field’s complete content in translation; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Interface ID (id). Original card: HV03. Interpretation: the short identifier within the cardbook tracks the same interface; it does not indicate that a real-world object has been proved. [T-main]","02 | Qualified ID (qualified_id). Original card: human_variable:HV03. Interpretation: the namespace prevents naming collisions; references to this ID must still carry object and evidential status. [T-main]","03 | Name (name). Original card: Orientation anchor. Interpretation: the name provides a reading entry point, not an automatic label for particular people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Claim type (claim_type). Original card: H. Interpretation: H marks human adaptation; it does not mean all six human clauses are the same kind of fact. R1 effects in this interface require independent H1-instance support; the interface itself carries no empirical truth value. [T-main]","05 | Contract role (contract_role). Original card: human_variable_interface. Interpretation: this variable-interface contract specifies how to enter analysis; a field’s existence does not mean an empirical instance has been established. [T-main]","06 | Proposition (proposition). Original card: Goals, identities, memories, commitments, fears, or common problems may first be registered as candidate meaning materials; only when H1-instance supports preselected resource-allocation, action-choice, or coordination outcomes may a conditionally effective orientation anchor be registered within that instance’s scope. Interpretation: read this proposition together with the subsequent conditional routes and ceilings; do not extract an unconditional causal or normative conclusion from one sentence. [T-main]","07 | Scope (scope). Original card: Human structures with intention, coordination, or common problems. Interpretation: scope lists task domains that may be studied, not empirical support for every object within them. [T-main]","08 | Pause condition (pause_condition). Original card: Where there are only slogans, interpreter projection, or coerced uniform expression, do not register an R1 conditionally effective orientation anchor; sourced expressive materials, heterogeneous expressions, and coercion risks may still be registered under R0, without inferring a collective will. Interpretation: pausing R1 does not delete expressive evidence. Slogans or forced agreement alone do not register an effective anchor, but sourced materials, heterogeneity, and coercion risks may remain under R0. [T-main]","09 | Inferential dependencies (inferential_requires). Original card: 1. human_variable:HV01. Interpretation: inferential dependencies provide upstream content needed for the proposition to hold; upstream candidate/support status must pass through unchanged, without automatic upgrading by citation. [T-main]","10 | Protocol dependencies (protocol_requires). Original card: 1. E2; 2. EVIDENCE; 3. SOURCE. Interpretation: protocol dependencies constrain evidence gathering and expression; completing protocols does not independently prove an empirical proposition true. [T-main]","11 | Qualification / specialization (specializes). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: an empty set means only that no such specialization relations are registered, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a nonempty set also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Applicable-object references (applies_to). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: applicable-object references cannot replace the current object contract; an empty set is explicit registration, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Conditional support routes (conditional_support_routes). Original card: 1. route_id=HV03-R0-candidate-anchor; claim_level=candidate_description; when=Goals, identities, memories, commitments, fears, or common problems have traceable expressions and forms of carriage, but no eligible H1-instance exists yet.; additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set); additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set); allowed_conclusion=Register candidate meaning materials, heterogeneous expressions, representativeness disputes, and H1 supplementary-evidence needs.; result_ceiling=Candidate expressions of meaning only; do not call them effective orientation anchors or collective will. 2. route_id=HV03-R1-effective-anchor; claim_level=conditional_effect; when=A preregistered H1-instance obtains supported on the uniquely preselected criterion among resource-allocation, action-choice, or coordination outcomes.; additional_inferential_requires=H1-instance; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4; allowed_conclusion=Register a conditionally effective orientation anchor within that instance, outcome family, scale, and window.; result_ceiling=Do not extrapolate to unselected resource, action, or coordination outcomes, or infer genuine consent, unified inner states, or coercively unified meaning. Interpretation: R0 retains traceable meaning expressions and heterogeneous disputes; R1 must uniquely preselect an outcome criterion among resources, actions, and coordination, obtain eligible H1 supported, and add CAUSAL/E4, without switching endpoints afterward. [T-main]","14 | Allowed inference (allowed_inference). Original card: 1. Conditional coordination directions and conflict anchors. Interpretation: coordination directions or conflict anchors may be judged only within the corresponding route’s scope; candidate expressions are not R1 effects and do not automatically have normative justification. [T-main]","15 | Prohibited leap (prohibited_leap). Original card: 1. A group has a unified inner mind; 2. Shared language equals genuine consent; 3. The goal is justified. Interpretation: these are common boundary violations, not avoided by rewording; inspect actual inference and downstream use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale profile (scale_profile). Original card: SP=<A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J>; A=aggregation level: individual expressions or actions, subject cases, candidate participant populations, distributions of positions, and aggregation rules; X=spatial scope: relational sites, organizational spaces, digital public spaces, and cross-domain dissemination; T=time span: expression–action windows, commitment cycles, and drift/decoupling lags; O=organizational level: action roles, teams, organizations, institutions, through public-governance ecosystems; C=causal level: expression events, meaning–action interaction mechanisms, meso-level coordination structures, institutional arrangements, and systemic conditions; R=observational resolution: original expressions and actions, event sequences, cases, distributions of positions, coordination indicators, and summaries, with compression loss registered; I=sphere of influence: direct participants, dissenters, indirectly affected people, second-order coordination consequences, cross-domain and intergenerational effects; N=network-topological scope: dissemination of expressions, coordination connections, heterogeneous clusters, and bridging nodes; J=jurisdiction and authorization scope: register authorization separately for anchor naming, adoption of representativeness, and coordination or unification demands; heterogeneous anchors must be retained. Interpretation: register all nine axes individually; particularly, sphere of influence I creates no jurisdictional authority J, and lower observational resolution R requires retention of compression loss. [T-main]","17 | Effective object (effective_object). Original card: Goals, identities, memories, commitments, fears, or common problems that may alter resource allocation, action choice, or coordination outcomes. Interpretation: this describes the interface’s corresponding object; choosing a lower-threshold descriptive route cannot treat functional meaning in the name as established causation. [T-main]","18 | Cross-scale invariants (scale_invariants). Original card: 1. Bridges from meaning to preselected resource-allocation, action-choice, or coordination outcomes. Interpretation: invariants are comparative responsibilities retained during transfer, not a declaration that variable values remain constant at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Required additions on elevation (required_scale_additions). Original card: 1. Representation rules; 2. Heterogeneity; 3. Cost/benefit distributions; 4. J axis. Interpretation: elevation is not copying and enlarging the original table. Where materials on new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and so on are incomplete, restrict conclusions dependent on them. [T-main]","20 | Semantics changing with scale (changing_semantics). Original card: 1. Anchor content, strength, and carrying subjects may change. Interpretation: the same word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; reconstruct correspondence rather than assume the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Inapplicable objects (non_applicable_objects). Original card: 1. Nonhuman systems without capability for intention, meaning, or commitment. Interpretation: inapplicability constraints prevent unconditional projection of human normative, responsibility, and experiential interfaces; they do not deny that other theories can study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Forbidden elevation (forbidden_elevation). Original card: 1. Directly upgrading local expressions into collective will. Interpretation: inference from local to whole requires separate proof; broad impact, a grand name, or formal similarity cannot supply representativeness and authorization. [T-main]","23 | State set (state). Original card: 1. Dispersed; 2. Cohesive; 3. Competing; 4. Rigidified; 5. Decoupled. Interpretation: states are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people; state changes require windows, grounds, and versions. [T-main]","24 | Observables (observables). Original card: 1. Resource-allocation differences before/after preregistered meaning expressions appear; 2. Changes in action choices, collaboration completion rates, or conflict patterns; 3. Acceptance, refusal, and alternative formulations of anchors at different positions; 4. Whether outcome differences under comparison conditions exceed predetermined thresholds. Interpretation: observable indications tell us where to seek materials; a single indication’s presence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements (evidence). Original card: 1. Resource adjustments; 2. Action sequences; 3. Commitment and exit records; 4. Conflict evidence. Interpretation: resource, action, commitment/exit, and conflict materials may have different uses; applying for H1 requires keeping the preselected outcome family rather than retrospectively seeking the most favorable endpoint among them. [T-main]","26 | Input dependencies and interface content (input_dependencies). Original card: 1. Participating positions; 2. Safety of expression; 3. Resource and action data. Interpretation: this states what arrives from upstream and its eligibility; gaps cannot quietly disappear while passing between interfaces. [T-main]","27 | Output effects and variable flow (output_effects). Original card: 1. Generative events; 2. Mobilization for uptake; 3. Normative-choice agendas. Interpretation: variable flow identifies downstream judgments that may depend on this interface; passing references guarantees neither downstream conclusions, actual repair, nor authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag (time_window_and_lag). Original card: Distinguish short-term slogans, long-term commitments, and intergenerational memories. Interpretation: occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may differ in time; short-term materials cannot automatically certify extensions beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty (uncertainty). Original card: Record silence, coerced agreement, and internal heterogeneity. Interpretation: direct uncertainty toward specific materials and positions; an overall confidence score cannot conceal fundamentally uncovered positions. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone (local_exclusion_zone). Original card: Different goals at low-safety positions must not be erased by aggregation. Interpretation: exclusion zones limit current inferential coverage, not delete people or facts from the world; unavailability for safety is neither consent nor absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions (affected_positions). Original card: 1. Those identifying with the anchor; 2. Dissenters; 3. Those represented; 4. Cost bearers. Interpretation: register directly and indirectly affected parties together; visible members do not represent every cost bearer. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carrier (carrier). Original card: 1. Narratives; 2. Commitments; 3. Shared memories; 4. Institutional goals; 5. Problem definitions. Interpretation: a carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something; its name, completed construction, and arrival of the required function are different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible party (responsible_subject). Original card: 1. Those claiming representativeness; 2. Those allocating resources on that basis. Interpretation: identify who proposes, operates, or uses judgments; recording responsibility is not automatically a legal-liability adjudication or new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status (normative_status). Original card: An anchor’s existence does not prove it justified. Interpretation: distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status according to the card. Established facts do not independently determine value rankings; normative reasons do not automatically prove empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling (judgment_ceiling). Original card, translated from Chinese: Limit R0 and R1 separately: R0 only describes candidate expressions of meaning; when the corresponding H1-instance supports the selected outcome effect, R1 permits a bounded conditional-anchor explanation, without extrapolating to unselected outcomes. Interpretation: R0 identifies only candidate expressions; R1 is confined to the selected outcome effect. Improved coordination cannot establish improved resources or genuine consent. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling (action_ceiling). Original card, translated from Chinese: This variable generates only descriptions of candidate anchors, heterogeneous expressions, comparison results, and evidence needs; it does not authorize unifying meanings, representing wishes, or coordinating action. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass C12, explicit runtime N premises, J authorization, and O procedures. Interpretation: The interface itself creates no real-world authority to act; actual actions separately check choice, C12, explicit N, valid J, O, protection, and duration. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples (counterexamples). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Repeated slogans change no resource allocation or action; 2. Uniform expression under high pressure conceals conflicting actual objectives. Interpretation: Counterexamples limit unconditional extrapolation. They may require narrowing the scope or collecting evidence again, without automatically negating all bounded uses. [T-main]","38 | Appeal (appeal). Original card, translated from Chinese: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, members may deny representativeness, submit heterogeneous objectives, or refuse to be anchored through safely reachable, anti-retaliation channels, triggering review independent of the original anchor-judgment chain. Interpretation: Appeals must be safely reachable, protected against retaliation, and able to trigger independent review; a channel name alone does not establish power to change an error. [T-main]","39 | Rollback (rollback). Original card, translated from Chinese: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, once rollback is authorized, a designated responsible person must actually revoke the anchor designation, remove its representative and downstream coordination effects, and restore the state of heterogeneous expression within the stipulated time, preserving versions and completion verification. Interpretation: Within valid authorization, actually revoke erroneous effects, correct downstream consequences, and verify completion. Changing document wording does not mean rights and resources have been restored to the person concerned. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Fix the object, sources of expression, carrier forms, and observation positions.","R0 registers candidate meanings, heterogeneous expressions, disputes about representation, and evidence needs.","When testing R1, first uniquely preselect one outcome family—resources/action/coordination—and its criteria.","Use qualified H1, CAUSAL, and E4 to judge instance effects, keeping unresolved and null conclusions distinct.","Restrict claims to the selected outcome window; do not infer consent, a common will, or normative justification.","Protect those refusing representation; after authorization, revoke erroneous downstream representative/coordination effects."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Expression of meaning","leftText":"Sourced objectives, fears, or commitments.","rightTitle":"Effective anchor","rightText":"A specified H1 instance supports the preselected outcome effect."},{"leftTitle":"Common language","leftText":"People visibly say the same words.","rightTitle":"Genuine consent","rightText":"Requires checking heterogeneity, coercion, and free choice."},{"leftTitle":"Coordination effect","leftText":"A particular outcome changes under specified conditions.","rightTitle":"A justified objective","rightText":"Normative reasons and protection require separate judgment."},{"leftTitle":"The positive gate was not passed","leftText":"Unsupported or unresolved.","rightTitle":"Null conclusion","rightText":"Requires meeting a separately specified null-conclusion check; it cannot be automatically expanded."}],"boundaries":["Original-card counterexample: repeated slogans change neither resource allocation nor action.","Original-card counterexample: uniform expression under high pressure conceals conflicting objectives.","Teaching counterexample: after a preselected resource outcome fails, switching to an attractive coordination metric to declare success.","Boundary in the source: local expressions cannot be directly elevated into a common will."],"questions":["Does a repeatedly occurring slogan constitute an effective anchor?","Does improved coordination establish that an objective is justified?","When coerced uniformity is found, should even the expressions go unrecorded?","Can another favorable outcome rescue a failed preselected outcome?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/figures/hv03-routes.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The 1990 ADA signing statement as a traceable expression","href":"https://www.archives.gov/research/americans-with-disabilities/transcriptions/naid-6037493-statement-by-the-president-americans-with-disabilities-act-of-1990.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The 1990 ADA signing statement as a traceable expression","text":"The presidential statement of 1990-07-26 publicly expresses ideals of freedom and equality, administrative support, and commitments to implementation; the archives preserve its transcript.","sourceLabel":"US National Archives: Statement By The President, July 26, 1990","sourceUrl":"https://www.archives.gov/research/americans-with-disabilities/transcriptions/naid-6037493-statement-by-the-president-americans-with-disabilities-act-of-1990.html","sourceLocator":"The statement's opening paragraph and final two body paragraphs, webpage lines 27 and 41–42","boundary":"The text establishes only the expression's content and timing, not fulfillment of commitments, H1 effects, public consent, or full justification of the objectives.","href":"https://www.archives.gov/research/americans-with-disabilities/transcriptions/naid-6037493-statement-by-the-president-americans-with-disabilities-act-of-1990.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.3","start_line":3877,"end_line":3942,"startLine":3877,"endLine":3942}],"outputId":"section-a-3","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: three outcomes cannot be selected for the best result afterward","text":"A constructed study preselects resource allocation alone as its primary outcome. It later observes a resource difference of 0, an action-metric difference of 2, and a coordination-metric difference of 8. The apparent size of coordination's 8 cannot justify renaming the original resource test a successful H1. Coordination material may generate a separately preregistered candidate hypothesis; whether the resource result supports a null effect still requires its dedicated null-conclusion check, and a difference of 0 alone is insufficient.","assumptions":"Numbers, metric scales, and the study are teaching constructions; no formal statistical determination is supplied.","boundary":"Failure to support one primary outcome does not negate every effect of meaning. What is prohibited is changing the primary target after results are known."},{"title":"Teaching construction: different objectives beneath uniform expression","text":"In a fictional meeting, everyone says “protect the project.” A hopes to keep a job, B hopes to stop a dangerous function on schedule, and C fears retaliation for dissent. Shared wording and heterogeneous interpretations may be registered, without inferring unified inner intentions from identical wording. Nor may C be forced to publicly prove their situation before a safety gap is recognized.","assumptions":"Constructed people and situations; no inference about actual motives.","boundary":"Different interpretations require support from the person concerned or independent material; analysts cannot project actual inner states themselves."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: repeated slogans change neither resource allocation nor action.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: uniform expression under high pressure conceals conflicting objectives.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: after a preselected resource outcome fails, switching to an attractive coordination metric to declare success.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: local expressions cannot be directly elevated into a common will.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does a repeatedly occurring slogan constitute an effective anchor?","answer":"It suffices only to register expression under qualified sourcing; R1 requires corresponding H1 support for the preselected outcome.","basis":"R0/R1。"},{"question":"Does improved coordination establish that an objective is justified?","answer":"No. Effects and normative standing remain separate.","basis":"normative_status and prohibited leaps."},{"question":"When coerced uniformity is found, should even the expressions go unrecorded?","answer":"Record sourced expressions and coercion risks, but do not register an R1 effective anchor or a common will.","basis":"pause_condition。"},{"question":"Can another favorable outcome rescue a failed preselected outcome?","answer":"The outcome family cannot be changed after the event; new discoveries retain exploratory status.","basis":"The H1 contract and R1."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/figures/hv03-routes.png","alt":"R0 candidate expression; R1 preselects one of resources/action/coordination and receives H1 supported status; no extrapolation to other outcomes.","caption":"Based on A.3 and the human empirical-instance contract in §4.2."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/figures/hv03-meaning.png","alt":"Shared wording does not establish shared inner states, and effects do not establish justification; heterogeneous objectives and refusal of representation must remain.","caption":"Based on A.3's protection, normative, and rollback fields."}],"diagramBrief":"The first diagram fixes R0/R1 and the uniquely preselected outcome; the second distinguishes expression, effects, consent, and justification. Arrows indicate evidentiary responsibilities, not a compulsory process of shared meaning.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No actual H1-instance was constructed; there is no causal estimate or formal supported determination.","The representativeness, coercion risks, and heterogeneous objectives of real meaning-related material require safe evidence collection; analysts cannot project them."],"taskId":"S-A-3","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.3, Chinese source lines 3700–3765","claim_support":["All 39 HV03 fields, R0/R1, and protection of heterogeneous expression."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-H1-contract","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.2, Chinese source lines 586–601","claim_support":["H1's uniquely preselected outcome family and positive and null-conclusion checks."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-ADA","title":"US National Archives: Statement By The President, July 26, 1990","url":"https://www.archives.gov/research/americans-with-disabilities/transcriptions/naid-6037493-statement-by-the-president-americans-with-disabilities-act-of-1990.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1990-07-26","locator":"The statement's opening paragraph and final two body paragraphs, webpage lines 27 and 41–42","claim_support":["The 1990 signing statement publicly invokes freedom and equality, administrative support, and commitments to implementation."],"limits":["I read the complete statement transcript. This discussion relies only on the expression itself; it does not establish genuine public consent, fulfillment of commitments, or causal effects of expressions of meaning, and offers no current legal advice."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"A.3 HV03 Orientation Anchor (Complete Interface Card); Chinese source lines 3786–3851; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/source/S-A-3.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"db42c65fc2275e7b43a09b39098ef09e7f69250bcbb4af047130e1b163c3ed4e","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-03.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-03.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-03.md"};function l(p,e,u,m,h,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return o(),a("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV03 Orientation Anchor (Full Interface Card) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.3 HV03 Orientation Anchor (Full Interface Card) {#section-a-3}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">HV03 retains sourced expressions of meaning and heterogeneous goals; only support for a preselected H1 outcome permits registration of a bounded effective anchor. Effects prove neither shared inner states nor goal justification.</span><details class="xk-gist__codes"><summary>Codes used in this section (5)</summary><ul><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-3"><b>HV03</b>Orientation Anchor</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-1"><b>HV01</b>Structural Domain</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-8-2"><b>E2</b>Observation Position and Model Limits</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-8-4"><b>E4</b>Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-14-1"><b>C12</b>Check on the Validity of Normative Bridging</a></li></ul></details></div><p>A. Identity, Proposition, and Scope</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interface ID (<code>id</code>)</td><td>HV03</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified ID (<code>qualified_id</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable:HV03</code></td></tr><tr><td>Name (<code>name</code>)</td><td>Orientation Anchor</td></tr><tr><td>Claim type (<code>claim_type</code>)</td><td>H</td></tr><tr><td>Contract role (<code>contract_role</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable_interface</code></td></tr><tr><td>Proposition (<code>proposition</code>)</td><td>A goal, identity, memory, commitment, fear, or common problem may first be registered as candidate material about meaning. Register a conditionally effective orientation anchor within the scope of the instance only if an H1-instance provides support on the preselected outcome for resource allocation, action choice, or coordination.</td></tr><tr><td>Scope (<code>scope</code>)</td><td>Human structures involving intentionality, coordination, or common problems</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions for suspension (<code>pause_condition</code>)</td><td>Do not register a conditionally effective orientation anchor under R1 when there are only slogans, an interpreter’s projections, or coerced uniformity of expression. Sourced expressions, diverse expressions, and risks of coercion may still be registered under R0. Do not infer a common will from them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>B. Formal Dependencies and Limits on Conclusions</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inferential dependencies (<code>inferential_requires</code>)</td><td>1. <code>human_variable:HV01</code></td></tr><tr><td>Protocol dependencies (<code>protocol_requires</code>)</td><td>1. E2<br>2. EVIDENCE<br>3. SOURCE</td></tr><tr><td>Qualification/specialization (<code>specializes</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>References to applicable objects (<code>applies_to</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional support routes (<code>conditional_support_routes</code>)</td><td>1. route_id=HV03-R0-candidate-anchor;<br>claim_level=candidate_description;<br>when=A goal, identity, memory, commitment, fear, or common problem has traceable ways of being expressed and carried, but there is not yet an eligible H1-instance;<br>additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);<br>additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);<br>allowed_conclusion=Register candidate material about meaning, diverse expressions, disputes over representativeness, and the need for further H1 evidence;<br>result_ceiling=Candidate expressions of meaning only. Do not call them an effective orientation anchor or a common will.<br>2. route_id=HV03-R1-effective-anchor;<br>claim_level=conditional_effect;<br>when=A pre-registered H1-instance returns supported on the single criterion selected in advance among resource allocation, action choice, and coordination outcomes;<br>additional_inferential_requires=H1-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a conditionally effective orientation anchor within the instance, outcome family, scale, and window;<br>result_ceiling=Do not extrapolate to unselected resource, action, or coordination outcomes. Do not infer genuine consent or a unified inner state, or treat the result as grounds for imposing uniformity of meaning.</td></tr><tr><td>Permitted inferences (<code>allowed_inference</code>)</td><td>1. Conditional directions of coordination and anchors of conflict</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited leaps (<code>prohibited_leap</code>)</td><td>1. Attributing a unified inner state to a group<br>2. Equating shared language with genuine consent<br>3. Assuming the goal is normatively justified</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>C. Nine-Axis Scale and Object Contract</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nine-axis scale profile (<code>scale_profile</code>)</td><td>SP=&lt;A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J&gt;. A=Aggregation level covers individual expressions or actions, cases of particular subjects, the population of potential participants, distributions of stances, and aggregation rules. X=Spatial extent covers sites of relationships, organizational spaces, digital public spaces, and the reach of propagation across domains. T=Time span covers the window from expression to action, cycles of commitment, and lags in drift and decoupling. O=Organizational level covers roles in action, teams, organizations, institutions, and public governance ecologies. C=Causal level covers events of expression, mechanisms of interaction between meaning and action, meso-level coordination structures, institutional arrangements, and systemic conditions. R=Resolution covers original expressions and actions, event sequences, individual cases, distributions of stances, coordination metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. I=Scope of influence covers direct participants, dissenters, indirectly affected people, second-order consequences for coordination, and impacts across domains and generations. N=Network topology scope covers propagation of expressions, links for coordination, heterogeneous clusters, and bridging nodes. J=Scope of jurisdiction and authorization requires separate authorization records for naming the anchor, adopting a judgment of representativeness, and requiring coordination or uniformity. Diverse anchors must be preserved</td></tr><tr><td>Effective object (<code>effective_object</code>)</td><td>A goal, identity, memory, commitment, fear, or common problem that may change resource allocation, action choice, or coordination outcomes</td></tr><tr><td>Scale invariants (<code>scale_invariants</code>)</td><td>1. The bridge from meaning to the preselected resource allocation, action choice, or coordination outcome</td></tr><tr><td>Required additions for scale elevation (<code>required_scale_additions</code>)</td><td>1. Rules of representation<br>2. Heterogeneity<br>3. Distribution of costs and benefits<br>4. J axis</td></tr><tr><td>What changes with scale (<code>changing_semantics</code>)</td><td>1. The content and strength of an anchor and the subjects who carry it can change</td></tr><tr><td>Inapplicable objects (<code>non_applicable_objects</code>)</td><td>1. Nonhuman systems without intentionality, meaning, or capability for commitment</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited elevations (<code>forbidden_elevation</code>)</td><td>1. Treating local expression directly as a common will</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>D. State, Evidence, and Variable Flows</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State set (<code>state</code>)</td><td>1. Dispersed<br>2. Coalescing<br>3. Competing<br>4. Solidified<br>5. Decoupled</td></tr><tr><td>Observables (<code>observables</code>)</td><td>1. Differences in resource allocation before and after the pre-registered expression of meaning appears<br>2. Changes in action choices, rates of completion in collaboration, or conflict patterns<br>3. Acceptance, rejection, and alternative expressions of the anchor at different positions<br>4. Whether the outcome difference under the comparison conditions exceeds the predetermined threshold</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence requirements (<code>evidence</code>)</td><td>1. Resource adjustments<br>2. Sequences of action<br>3. Commitment and exit records<br>4. Evidence of conflict</td></tr><tr><td>Input dependencies and interface content (<code>input_dependencies</code>)</td><td>1. Positions of participation. 2. Safety of expression. 3. Data on resources and actions</td></tr><tr><td>Output effects and variable flows (<code>output_effects</code>)</td><td>1. Generative events. 2. Mobilization of uptake. 3. Agendas for normative choice</td></tr><tr><td>Time window and lag (<code>time_window_and_lag</code>)</td><td>Distinguish short-term slogans, long-term commitments, and intergenerational memory</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty (<code>uncertainty</code>)</td><td>Record silence, coerced agreement, and internal heterogeneity</td></tr><tr><td>Local exclusion zone (<code>local_exclusion_zone</code>)</td><td>Aggregation must not erase different goals at positions where people have limited safety</td></tr><tr><td>Affected positions (<code>affected_positions</code>)</td><td>1. People who identify with the anchor<br>2. Dissenters<br>3. People represented<br>4. Cost bearers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>E. Uptake, Responsibility, Normative Status, Ceilings, and Correction</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Uptake carriers (<code>carrier</code>)</td><td>1. Narratives<br>2. Commitments<br>3. Shared memory<br>4. Institutional goals<br>5. Problem definitions</td></tr><tr><td>Responsible parties (<code>responsible_subject</code>)</td><td>1. Those making claims of representativeness. 2. Those allocating resources on that basis</td></tr><tr><td>Normative status (<code>normative_status</code>)</td><td>Existence of an anchor does not establish that it is normatively justified</td></tr><tr><td>Judgment ceiling (<code>judgment_ceiling</code>)</td><td>Limit R0 and R1 separately. R0 only describes candidate expressions of meaning. When the corresponding H1-instance supports the effect on the selected outcome, R1 may explain a conditional anchor within a limited scope. Do not extrapolate to unselected outcomes</td></tr><tr><td>Action ceiling (<code>action_ceiling</code>)</td><td>This variable produces only descriptions of candidate anchors, diverse expressions, comparison results, and needs for more evidence. It does not authorize unifying meaning, representing others’ wishes, or coordinating action. Any real-world adjustment separately requires C12, explicit N premises at runtime, J authorization, and O procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Counterexamples (<code>counterexamples</code>)</td><td>1. A repeated slogan does not change any allocation of resources or action<br>2. Uniform expressions under high pressure conceal genuine goals that conflict</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal (<code>appeal</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, members can contest representativeness, submit different goals, or refuse to be anchored through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection. This triggers review independent of the original chain of judgment about the anchor</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback (<code>rollback</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, once rollback is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline actually revoke the naming of the anchor, remove its force as a representation and its downstream effect on coordination, and restore the state of diverse expression. Preserve versions and verification of completion</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"A.3"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const v=n(d,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,v as default};
